00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.620
NATHEN HARVEY: Welcome
to "Google Cloud OnAir,"

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.750
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.270
We are hosting
webinars daily, and you

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.640
are watching "The Dev
Community," a show where

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.940
Google Cloud experts from
the developer community share

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.690
perspectives about the Cloud
ecosystem and what's trending.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.030
My name is Nathen Harvey
and I'm a Developer Advocate

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.140
with Google Cloud.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.120
NICOLE FORSGREN: Hi,
I'm Nicole Forsgren

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.530
and I am the DORA Lead.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.130
And today, we are talking
about the Accelerate 2019

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.460
State of DevOps Report.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.495
So let's get started.

00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:13.620
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah, let's.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.560
So the Accelerate State of
DevOps Report, it's all about,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.830
how do you become an
elite DevOps organization?

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.090
NICOLE FORSGREN: Absolutely.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.340
NATHEN HARVEY: So what we're
going to talk about today

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.000
is this.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.850
First, DevOps is here to stay.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.460
Next, we'll look at some
of the 2019 benchmarks.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.890
We'll talk about how
to improve, what works

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.123
and what doesn't when
it comes to scaling,

00:01:34.123 --> 00:01:36.540
and then we'll leave you with
a few takeaways and probably

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.050
a reading list.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.570
So first, DevOps.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.710
What is DevOps?

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.010
Well, DevOps as a cultural
and technical movement

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.550
focused on delivering value
by simultaneously improving

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.100
velocity and stability.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.460
DevOps encourages
cross-team collaboration,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.920
shared incentives
and responsibilities,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.355
and an environment that
celebrates learning.

00:01:58.355 --> 00:02:00.480
NICOLE FORSGREN: That's
kind of a lot to decompose.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:00.980
So--

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:01.850
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.120
NICOLE FORSGREN: --there's
a lot to get into.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.287
So don't worry, we're going
to be talking about some

00:02:05.287 --> 00:02:06.570
of that today.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.320
The thing that I love
talking about-- when

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.487
we say that it's here to
stay, is that it has really

00:02:10.487 --> 00:02:11.560
crossed the chasm.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.300
So what we found
this year is we've

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.670
really kind of validated
what so many analysts

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.140
have been talking about.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.360
For anyone who dug
into the 2018 report,

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.130
we found that 17%
of the respondents

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.750
were elite performers.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.820
But if you take a look
at this chart here,

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.940
that's kind of a subset
of the high performers.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.020
Here's what we found.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.860
That subgroup broke out
into their own group.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.250
It's 20% of our
respondents this year.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.980
So that's pretty
significant, right?

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.660
They almost tripled.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.120
It really has crossed the chasm.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.270
We also see 23% of our
group is high performers,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:53.630
44% now are medium performers,
and 12% are low performers.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.458
Now, for any of you who are
kind of mapping this across,

00:02:56.458 --> 00:02:57.250
here's what we see.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.790
As I mentioned, elite
performers have almost tripled.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.770
That high performance
group has pulled across.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.720
Now, medium performers grew.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.757
Where did that growth come from?

00:03:06.757 --> 00:03:07.590
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.215
NICOLE FORSGREN: Low
performers shrunk.

00:03:09.215 --> 00:03:12.170
So many of them got better,
grew into high performers.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.772
Some of our high performers fell
back into medium performers.

00:03:15.772 --> 00:03:17.230
NATHEN HARVEY:
Nicole, why is that?

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.545
NICOLE FORSGREN: So we
have a few theories.

00:03:19.545 --> 00:03:20.920
In many of the
organizations I've

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.630
worked with, what
happens is some of them

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.010
are struggling with complexity.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.067
A few of them also
are sitting back.

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:29.150
NATHEN HARVEY: Well, sure.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.800
You said DevOps has
crossed the chasm.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.120
So I've made it.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.450
I'm DevOps now.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.240
Now I can stop.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.130
Ah, that was quite a journey.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.908
But now we've reached
the destination.

00:03:38.908 --> 00:03:39.950
NICOLE FORSGREN: Exactly.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.980
And that's not the way
continuous improvement works.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.650
You don't just get to
do a maturity model,

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.750
check it off your
box, and be done.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.755
We need to keep
improving, particularly

00:03:49.755 --> 00:03:51.380
when we work in things
like technology,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.710
where it's highly complex.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:53.460
NATHEN HARVEY: Ah.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.040
So this is also interesting.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.590
You mentioned that the elite
performers have broken out.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.720
So help me understand
a little bit more

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.620
about, what does that mean from
2018 and as we move into 2019?

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.340
NICOLE FORSGREN: I
love that question.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.197
So how do we even measure this?

00:04:07.197 --> 00:04:08.030
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.655
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Let's talk about this.

00:04:09.655 --> 00:04:12.955
So when we talk about
DevOps and performance,

00:04:12.955 --> 00:04:15.330
it's important that we're all
speaking the same language.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:19.040
So when I'm doing research,
it means measurement.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.390
So some of you may have
heard of four key metrics.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:25.368
This has become an
industry standard.

00:04:25.368 --> 00:04:26.660
These are the four key metrics.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.370
We're talking about
speed and stability.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.000
The speed metrics are
deployment frequency--

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.360
how often you're pushing code--

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.180
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.620
NICOLE FORSGREN: --lead
time for changes--

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.210
how long it takes
to go from code

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.920
commit to code
running in production.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.730
And then two stability metrics,
time to restore service--

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.970
how long it takes
code to come back up--

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.160
and change fail rate.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:49.910
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.470
So first, time to
restore service,

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.690
that's when there's an incident
or an outage or something

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:53.800
like that?

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.470
NICOLE FORSGREN: Exactly

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.920
NATHEN HARVEY: Some sort
of service disruption.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.250
How long does it take
us to get back to that?

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:57.600
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:57.890
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:58.598
That makes sense.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:01.530
Then change fail rate.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.030
I mean, I understand each of
those three words on their own,

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.080
but putting them
together, I don't

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.390
understand what you mean there.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.000
NICOLE FORSGREN: Exactly.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.470
And I'm glad you
asked that question.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.800
So change fail rate--
and by the way,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.630
you can dig into the report.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.130
We have lots of words there.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.600
But basically,
it's the percentage

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.140
of changes that fail.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.660
But it's not just
an epic failure.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.850
It's not when your systems
come all the way down.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.910
It's any type of code that
you push into production that

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.440
requires any type of attention.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.960
So if it's any type
of instability,

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.820
any type of incident,
and it's a percentage.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:29.150
OK?

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:30.320
NATHEN HARVEY: Gotcha, gotcha.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.778
So it doesn't necessarily mean
that we had to roll it back.

00:05:32.778 --> 00:05:35.726
But it does mean that some
human had to take action on that

00:05:35.726 --> 00:05:35.990
after the change went--

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.032
NICOLE FORSGREN: Exactly.

00:05:37.032 --> 00:05:37.550
Exactly.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:37.760
NATHEN HARVEY: All right.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.360
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now, the
really interesting thing,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.985
and the really
important thing to note,

00:05:40.985 --> 00:05:44.480
is that, for six years in a
row now, we've seen that speed

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.470
and stability, all four of
these metrics, go together.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:50.390
They move in tandem.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:51.260
They cluster.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.290
So that picture
that you just saw,

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.930
that's actually-- that comes
from a cluster analysis.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.963
So statistically,
all-- technical term--

00:05:59.963 --> 00:06:01.880
I take them, all these
four metrics, I throw--

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:02.755
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:06:02.755 --> 00:06:05.390
NICOLE FORSGREN: --them in the
hopper and I see what happens,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:06.890
how they group.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:10.790
And we have always seen that
these four grouped together.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.680
In the past, it's been
into three groups.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.270
This year, it fell
into four groups.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:14.690
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.460
NICOLE FORSGREN: Elite
performers-- statistically,

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.770
those four grouped together.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.257
And then we have kind of a gap.

00:06:19.257 --> 00:06:21.590
And then the four group
together and then we have a gap.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.382
And then the four group
together and a gap.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:25.830
And that's why we call
them different performance

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:26.330
profiles--

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:26.660
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.000
NICOLE FORSGREN: --elite,
high, medium, and low.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.290
So the faster you develop
code, the better stability

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.537
is, the higher reliability you
also have, which I think is--

00:06:36.537 --> 00:06:37.620
NATHEN HARVEY: Wait, wait.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.323
Nicole, so I get that
clustering here, but--

00:06:40.323 --> 00:06:41.240
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah?

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.070
NATHEN HARVEY: --do you mean
the elite performers are fast?

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.720
Or do you mean that the
elite performers are stable?

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:46.970
NICOLE FORSGREN: They're both.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.520
You don't have to sacrifice
speed for stability

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.080
or stability for speed.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:51.830
NATHEN HARVEY: Mm.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.430
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Now, here's why.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.830
When we think about this, if
we're pushing code more often,

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:58.490
we are pushing it
in smaller batches--

00:06:58.490 --> 00:06:59.240
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.490
NICOLE FORSGREN: --which
means that when our code hits

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.588
production, it's smaller.

00:07:03.588 --> 00:07:04.380
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.505
NICOLE FORSGREN: Let's
think about that in reverse.

00:07:06.505 --> 00:07:07.657
If I push code less often--

00:07:07.657 --> 00:07:08.490
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.865
NICOLE FORSGREN:
--it batches up.

00:07:09.865 --> 00:07:11.520
So I have a bigger
chunk of code.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.710
So I have a bigger chunk
of code hitting production,

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.200
which is a larger blast radius.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:17.870
Which means it has
a higher likelihood

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.700
of causing an incident.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.950
And when I do have
an incident, it

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.220
takes longer to bring
my code base back up.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.160
And it takes me longer
to fix because it's

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.605
harder for me to debug.

00:07:29.605 --> 00:07:30.480
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.200
So to be more stable,
we have to move faster.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.150
And to be faster, we
have to be more stable.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:35.360
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:36.560
NATHEN HARVEY: And those
things move together.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:37.430
NICOLE FORSGREN: And
they move together.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:37.930
NATHEN HARVEY: Oh.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.222
Now, that's really interesting.

00:07:39.222 --> 00:07:42.590
But how do you really find
those elite performers

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:43.852
within that cluster?

00:07:43.852 --> 00:07:45.560
NICOLE FORSGREN: With
those four numbers.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.070
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:48.420
NICOLE FORSGREN: And I do
that statistically with--

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:48.987
it's math.

00:07:48.987 --> 00:07:49.820
NATHEN HARVEY: Math.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.112
NICOLE FORSGREN: That cluster
analysis, math and stats.

00:07:52.112 --> 00:07:55.310
Now, what does that
look like, though?

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.110
So often people
want to take a look

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:58.590
and understand where they are.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.540
So this is our benchmark.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.190
And as I mentioned,
elite performers

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.770
are optimizing on
all four measures.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.230
So we can see the
elite performers

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:12.700
are pushing code multiple
times a day or on demand.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.920
The lead time for changes
is less than a day.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.500
They can get that code all
the way through the pipeline.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.450
Time to restore service
is less than an hour.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.020
And change fail rate
is between 0% and 15%.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.450
It's usually closer to
2% to 3% to 4% to 5%.

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:26.200
NATHEN HARVEY: Hm.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.870
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now, low
performers, in contrast.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:31.840
The deployment frequency is
between once per month and once

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:32.980
every six months.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:35.770
Lead time for changes is between
one month and six months.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.799
Time to restore service is
between one week and one month.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:42.610
And that change fail rate-- take
a look-- between 46% and 60%.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.770
NATHEN HARVEY: So that's
about half the time

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:46.782
I have to intervene
after I've pushed

00:08:46.782 --> 00:08:47.990
a change out into production.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:49.532
NICOLE FORSGREN: So
think about that.

00:08:49.532 --> 00:08:53.260
So much of your time is
spent debugging your own code

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.308
in production,
putting out fires.

00:08:55.308 --> 00:08:56.100
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.224
Is that usually planned for?

00:08:58.224 --> 00:08:59.500
NICOLE FORSGREN: Probably not.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:00.375
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:03.130
NICOLE FORSGREN: So think
about value creation.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.840
Whether it's in
features or building

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.575
out new infrastructure--

00:09:06.575 --> 00:09:07.450
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:10.097
NICOLE FORSGREN: --this
is the value of DevOps.

00:09:10.097 --> 00:09:10.930
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.795
And when you talk
about deployment

00:09:12.795 --> 00:09:14.170
frequency, and
especially looking

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:17.810
at that elite performers, you
say multiple deploys per day.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.270
So what if I'm not doing
multiple deploys per day?

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.600
Does that mean I can't
be an elite performer?

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:26.440
NICOLE FORSGREN: So what it
means is you probably aren't.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.340
And we should be
pushing towards that.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.660
Now, it does say on demand
and multiple deploys per day.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.430
On demand is important
there, because it's

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.500
the difference
between a business

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.030
decision and a
technical constraint.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.670
So if you can, but
you're just not,

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.040
we do see some
differences between--

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.190
in retail, you may push
to a pre-prod environment,

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.900
but you aren't
necessarily pushing

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:50.080
to an app in the app store,
because no users want

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.150
to be refreshing their
apps every single hour.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:52.890
NATHEN HARVEY: Right, right.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:53.150
OK.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:55.570
NICOLE FORSGREN: But again,
it's a business decision and not

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.500
a technical constraint.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:57.010
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:57.670
Gotcha.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:58.300
NICOLE FORSGREN:
And it's important,

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.130
because it's not
just for features.

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:03.240
It can be for compliance
and regulatory changes.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.240
It could be for patches.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:05.230
It can be for security.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:06.807
It enables so many things.

00:10:06.807 --> 00:10:07.640
NATHEN HARVEY: Nice.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:08.140
NICOLE FORSGREN: OK.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:08.800
Let's move on.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:08.940
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:09.860
NICOLE FORSGREN: There's
so much to cover.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:10.693
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:13.000
So what are these
numbers actually look

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.590
like when we put
them all together?

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:15.330
NICOLE FORSGREN: When
we put them together

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.580
and when we compare, we see that
our highest performers, when

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.090
we compare to our
lowest performers,

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.470
see 208 times more
frequent code deployments,

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:29.510
106 times faster lead time
from commit to deploy.

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:31.270
Now, what does that enable?

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:32.860
Like I said, it's
not just features.

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:33.970
It's stability.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:35.110
It's reliability.

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.340
It's security.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:41.578
So it's over 2,600 times
faster recovery from incidents.

00:10:41.578 --> 00:10:42.370
NATHEN HARVEY: Wow.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:43.953
NICOLE FORSGREN: It
enables stability.

00:10:43.953 --> 00:10:45.620
It's seven times lower
change fail rate.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.120
NATHEN HARVEY: I
mean, these numbers

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:48.245
are almost hard to believe.

00:10:48.245 --> 00:10:49.537
NICOLE FORSGREN: I know, right?

00:10:49.537 --> 00:10:51.880
But we've seen it several
years in a row now.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:53.550
NATHEN HARVEY: How long has
this report been running?

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:54.220
NICOLE FORSGREN: Six.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:54.950
NATHEN HARVEY: This
is the sixth year?

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:55.790
NICOLE FORSGREN: This
is the sixth year now.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.458
NATHEN HARVEY: And
the numbers, it

00:10:57.458 --> 00:10:59.500
sounds like they're probably
improving over time,

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:01.505
but they lay out
essentially the same.

00:11:01.505 --> 00:11:02.380
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:03.310
Exactly.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:04.685
NATHEN HARVEY:
That's incredible.

00:11:04.685 --> 00:11:08.160
Now, it's great that
you can recover fast

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.120
and that your change
file rate is low

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:12.578
and you're deploying fast,
but what about availability?

00:11:12.578 --> 00:11:14.620
NICOLE FORSGREN: Availability
is super important.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:16.620
And I'm glad you brought
this up because there

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.180
is this saying that I love
that, day one is short

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:20.300
and day two is long.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:21.310
NATHEN HARVEY: Mm.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:22.852
NICOLE FORSGREN:
And for those of you

00:11:22.852 --> 00:11:24.910
unfamiliar with it, it's
that we can build code,

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:27.640
we can make code, we can make
software relatively quickly.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:30.568
But we need to maintain it for
a really, really long time.

00:11:30.568 --> 00:11:32.110
And that's what
availability gets at.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.390
It's about the assertions
and the promises

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.955
that we make and keep
to our customers.

00:11:36.955 --> 00:11:38.330
And so availability
is important.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.600
We started researching
and measuring

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.390
availability and its
impacts last year in 2018.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.120
We measured it again this year
and found that, no surprise,

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.830
it's really important.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.840
And it's important for
a couple of reasons.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:55.060
In part, it helps us predict
organizational performance

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:57.513
better than software
delivery alone.

00:11:57.513 --> 00:11:58.930
Now, organizational
performance is

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.730
important from a
commercial standpoint

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.320
and a non-commercial standpoint.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.090
Everyone's like, Nicole, what?

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.437
Girl.

00:12:05.437 --> 00:12:06.520
That's my academic hat on.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:10.637
Commercial are things like
productivity, revenue, right?

00:12:10.637 --> 00:12:11.470
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:12.280
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Non-commercial are

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.488
things like effectiveness
and efficiency and customer

00:12:14.488 --> 00:12:15.650
satisfaction.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:18.550
So it just comes back
to delivering value.

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:20.270
NATHEN HARVEY: OK, great.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.998
So how do we think
about availability?

00:12:22.998 --> 00:12:24.040
This was a good question.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:24.220
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.450
This is a great question.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.440
And availability is, again,
about that long tail.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.350
And so I'd really
like to point people

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:33.533
to Google's SRE practices.

00:12:33.533 --> 00:12:35.200
NATHEN HARVEY: Oh,
and this is something

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.130
that I definitely know about.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:36.410
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yes.

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:38.980
NATHEN HARVEY: So Google's
SRE practices can really

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.210
help define availability.

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:42.520
And the thing that I love
about the SRE practices

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.470
when we talk about
them is it always

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:45.890
starts with the customer.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.760
So we're really thinking about,
what does the customer journey

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:49.480
look like?

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.340
What is the customer expecting?

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:54.640
Availability-- if your server is
running but your customer can't

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:56.500
complete the task
that they wanted,

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.600
is your service
really available?

00:12:58.600 --> 00:12:59.740
Probably not.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.168
So it all comes down to that
customer-oriented focus.

00:13:02.168 --> 00:13:04.210
We want to understand,
what are our customers are

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.520
trying to do with our services?

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.340
And to that end, the
SRE practices also

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:13.060
introduce these three different
dynamics or dimensions

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.110
that you can look at.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.750
The service level indicators,
service level objectives,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:20.007
and service level
agreements or SLAs.

00:13:20.007 --> 00:13:21.340
So let's just walk through them.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.530
And I'm going to sort
of countdown in reverse.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:24.795
So we'll go 3, 2, 1.

00:13:24.795 --> 00:13:26.920
We're going to start with
service level indicators.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.740
So a service level
indicator is an indicator

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.570
that your service
gives that says, hey,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.880
are your customers happy
or are your customers sad?

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:37.780
It's a good way to sort of
measure uptime, availability,

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:39.010
latency, things like this.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:40.748
Things that matter to customers.

00:13:40.748 --> 00:13:42.790
And then an objective, a
service level objective,

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:45.680
is a goal that we set
around that metric.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.440
So we want to make sure that
we're available n-percent

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:48.940
of the time.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.480
Maybe that's 99.9% of the time.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.460
And maybe our latency--

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:58.280
are services responding
fast when we expect it to?

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:00.250
And then a service
level agreement kind of

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:01.750
goes a step beyond that.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:03.970
Now, a service level
agreement is typically

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.640
an arrangement that is
made between businesses.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.250
And so when you miss your
service level agreement,

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:10.840
there are usually consequences.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:14.420
Those consequences usually
are a refund to your customer.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.430
And I don't really know
what business you're in,

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:18.470
but I bet it's not giving
your customers money.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:19.720
NICOLE FORSGREN: Probably not.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:20.040
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:20.710
Probably not.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.585
We want to take the
money from the customers.

00:14:22.585 --> 00:14:24.660
I mean, we want to earn
that money, obviously.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.260
NICOLE FORSGREN: And we want
to make our customers happy.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:27.280
NATHEN HARVEY:
Absolutely, and so when

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:29.650
we look at service level
objectives and service level

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:33.160
indicators, it's really about
what makes that customer happy.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:34.860
NICOLE FORSGREN: Absolutely.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:35.360
OK.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:39.460
Now, this is some fun stats.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.050
This is some stats nerd time.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:41.260
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah?

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.135
NICOLE FORSGREN: But I
love this because it's

00:14:43.135 --> 00:14:46.177
really, really interesting from
a practitioner point of view.

00:14:46.177 --> 00:14:47.510
From an executive point of view.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:48.802
From an industry point of view.

00:14:48.802 --> 00:14:51.640
So this year, we ran
additional analysis-- well,

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.890
for the last few years, we've
run additional analysis.

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:56.813
And for years, we found the
industry doesn't matter.

00:14:56.813 --> 00:14:58.480
If anyone's ever heard
me talk, I always

00:14:58.480 --> 00:14:59.750
say industry doesn't matter.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:00.250
Guess what?

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:02.140
For the first time,
industry matters.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:02.890
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:04.590
So first, industry
doesn't matter.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:04.920
Now, industry does matter.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.730
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now,
industry matters.

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:10.900
NATHEN HARVEY: And so what you
mean as that industry can help

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.550
us understand if
this organization is

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:15.700
going to be an elite
performer or a low performer?

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.870
NICOLE FORSGREN: Or if
industry may have an impact.

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:18.330
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:18.830
OK.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.890
NICOLE FORSGREN: So for
an example, for years,

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.345
people have always said, oh,
but I'm highly regulated.

00:15:23.345 --> 00:15:24.220
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:25.178
NICOLE FORSGREN: Right?

00:15:25.178 --> 00:15:27.210
And I've always said, eh.

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:28.460
NATHEN HARVEY: Doesn't matter.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:29.180
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Don't make excuses.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.030
NATHEN HARVEY: Mm, OK.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:30.980
NICOLE FORSGREN: I
mean, I love you.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:32.510
Also, don't make excuses.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:36.125
So we capture industry
for a dozen industries.

00:15:36.125 --> 00:15:38.000
This is just kind of a
representative sample.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:38.510
NATHEN HARVEY: Uh-huh.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:39.530
NICOLE FORSGREN:
What we have found--

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:40.697
NATHEN HARVEY: Let me guess.

00:15:40.697 --> 00:15:41.960
Let me guess.

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.840
My guess is that the
technology organizations,

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.752
because they're
technology organizations,

00:15:46.752 --> 00:15:48.460
they're going to be
the elite performers.

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:52.550
They're going to be the
ones that really stand out.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:54.340
NICOLE FORSGREN: Everyone, no.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.050
NATHEN HARVEY: What?

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.130
NICOLE FORSGREN: Retail.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:01.230
And retail does
significantly better.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.328
Now, we lay this
out in the report--

00:16:03.328 --> 00:16:04.370
I love your shocked face.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.490
This is my favorite.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:10.000
Now, what we found is that,
for the last decade or so,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.810
there's been what has been
termed the retail apocalypse.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:13.810
NATHEN HARVEY: Oh, yeah.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:17.290
NICOLE FORSGREN: It is
unbelievably competitive.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.920
Retailers have had
to embrace things

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:23.800
like A/B testing to
really understand

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.555
what drives consumer behavior--

00:16:26.555 --> 00:16:27.430
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.972
NICOLE FORSGREN: --so that they
know what to offer consumers.

00:16:29.972 --> 00:16:32.020
They've had unbelievably
slim margin,

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:34.720
so they have to
embrace efficiency.

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:37.000
They have to embrace automation.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.720
They also have to do
things like scaling

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:43.295
for things like Black Friday.

00:16:43.295 --> 00:16:44.170
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.630
NICOLE FORSGREN: So there
is no question about,

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:48.130
should we go to the cloud?

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:49.990
Should we embrace automation?

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.940
Should we be doing
things like A/B testing?

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:53.290
They are already there.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.930
Because if they don't, they
have already closed down.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:57.805
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:16:57.805 --> 00:17:00.820
And with Google Cloud, we see
eBay doing things like this.

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:02.980
So they're using
machine learning models

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:04.690
and they're using
artificial intelligence

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.510
to really help their customers
find exactly what they're

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.310
looking for much faster.

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:11.530
Making that a much more
efficient transaction,

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.213
both from the
customer perspective

00:17:13.213 --> 00:17:14.380
and from eBay's perspective.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:15.255
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:17:15.255 --> 00:17:19.119
And so we caution that very many
other industries are probably

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:20.776
following suit pretty quickly.

00:17:20.776 --> 00:17:21.609
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:22.300
That's nice.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:23.675
NICOLE FORSGREN:
So another thing

00:17:23.675 --> 00:17:25.480
that we found is that
heavyweight process,

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.970
heavyweight controls,
heavyweight change approval

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:29.173
boards--

00:17:29.173 --> 00:17:29.965
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:17:29.965 --> 00:17:31.090
NICOLE FORSGREN:
--only slow you down.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.465
NATHEN HARVEY:
They slow us down.

00:17:32.465 --> 00:17:34.390
NICOLE FORSGREN: And,
as we've mentioned,

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:36.460
when you are slowed
down, that leads

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.505
to batching up of work,
which leads to instability.

00:17:39.505 --> 00:17:40.380
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.780
So a heavyweight
process and control

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:46.420
over the changes that go in,
that's there for stability.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:46.730
NICOLE FORSGREN: It is.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.460
NATHEN HARVEY: It's there
to help us and protect us.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.590
NICOLE FORSGREN: We have
the best of intentions.

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:54.020
But that can lead to
unintended consequences.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:56.620
And in 2014, we studied
this for the first time.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.690
And we found that
heavyweight change approvals

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:01.300
were negatively
correlated with stability.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.833
And so, this year, we took
a more rigorous approach.

00:18:04.833 --> 00:18:06.250
I wanted to really
design this in.

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:08.680
And what we find
is it negatively

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:13.313
predicts speed and stability
for the same reason

00:18:13.313 --> 00:18:14.230
I talked about before.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:14.470
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:16.262
NICOLE FORSGREN: When
you batch up changes,

00:18:16.262 --> 00:18:18.860
you have a larger blast radius.

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:21.400
So it causes instability.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:24.880
NATHEN HARVEY: But so, in my
organization, I'm an architect.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:26.320
And I have a good eye--

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.890
I can see the entire landscape
and I understand how changes

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:31.360
are coming at the system.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.570
And I have a better purview
to decide whether or not

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:35.860
this is a safe change.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:37.840
