--and kind of forget

00:26:10.580 --> 00:26:12.600
that the idea is also
to scale it back down.

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:13.210
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:13.980
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:14.480
Well--

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:16.910
NICOLE FORSGREN: Which
relates to measured service.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:17.330
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:18.920
So we want to measure,
how much are we

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.260
actually using the cloud?

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:24.650
And we can get way
different insights

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:27.500
into how we're
utilizing a service.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.941
And, importantly, what my CFO
cares about, how much money

00:26:29.941 --> 00:26:31.066
am I spending on a service?

00:26:31.066 --> 00:26:31.410
That's a completely different--

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:31.760
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:33.300
And we only pay for what we use.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:33.710
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:33.995
NICOLE FORSGREN: Absolutely.

00:26:33.995 --> 00:26:34.860
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:38.090
So this is what defines cloud.

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:43.650
So-- but, listen, cloud's
been around for a long time.

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:46.310
So basically every--

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:48.260
I mean, shouldn't it
just be a control?

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:49.820
Obviously, everyone's
doing cloud.

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:50.840
NICOLE FORSGREN:
In theory, right?

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:51.110
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.250
NICOLE FORSGREN: The
challenge, though, like I said,

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:53.970
is that everyone
defines it differently.

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:55.970
So that's why this
research is so important.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.025
Because when I go
talk to organizations,

00:26:58.025 --> 00:27:00.650
when I go talk to people leading
transformations, they're like,

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:01.650
but we did the cloud.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:03.030
I'm not getting benefits.

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:03.980
Why doesn't it work?

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:04.855
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:27:04.855 --> 00:27:07.520
NICOLE FORSGREN: So we
asked, who's in the cloud?

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.230
And then we asked
these five questions.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:12.080
Turns out, only
29% of respondents

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:14.540
who said they're
using a cloud actually

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:17.550
meet all five characteristics
of cloud computing.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.413
So if you're in an
organization that says,

00:27:19.413 --> 00:27:20.330
we're doing the cloud.

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:21.330
It's not working for me.

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:22.630
I'm not getting thing--

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:22.880
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.840
NICOLE FORSGREN: --this
is the disconnect.

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:26.420
So it requires execution.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:29.660
I mean, I bought--

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:33.910
I mean, I paid for a
membership to my gym,

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:37.278
but I don't have a five
mile-- a five minute mile yet.

00:27:37.278 --> 00:27:39.570
NATHEN HARVEY: I paid for a
membership to the gym also.

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:40.460
NICOLE FORSGREN: [LAUGH]

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:41.293
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:27:41.293 --> 00:27:41.820
Mhm.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.090
NICOLE FORSGREN: You
have to do the work.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:44.850
I'm not saying it's
necessarily simple.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:45.570
It requires work.

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:46.590
It requires automation.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.480
It requires architecting things.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:48.660
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:49.620
NICOLE FORSGREN: But
you've got to do them both.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:50.460
You've got to do them both.

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:51.700
NATHEN HARVEY: And
investment, it sounds like.

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:51.920
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:52.820
NATHEN HARVEY: So--

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:53.320
OK.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:55.920
So clearly there's room
to grow with cloud.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.647
But how does this
line up across--

00:27:57.647 --> 00:27:58.980
NICOLE FORSGREN: Why do we care?

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:00.960
NATHEN HARVEY: --elite
performers, low performers,

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:01.500
what does that show?

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.542
NICOLE FORSGREN: It enables
and accelerates this.

00:28:03.542 --> 00:28:05.190
Elite performers
are 24 times more

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:08.130
likely to have met all
cloud characteristics.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:08.940
NATHEN HARVEY: Wow.

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:09.360
NICOLE FORSGREN: Right?

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:09.750
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.400
NICOLE FORSGREN: Because
it's about enabling.

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:12.520
It's about accelerating.

00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:13.988
It's about speeding things up.

00:28:13.988 --> 00:28:14.780
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:17.440
NICOLE FORSGREN: It really
helps so many things go.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:18.800
OK.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:20.150
We've got some clear wins here.

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:20.983
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:28:20.983 --> 00:28:22.880
It's clear that we need
to get to the cloud--

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:25.063
and really think about the
cloud as the whole thing.

00:28:25.063 --> 00:28:25.980
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:27.950
NATHEN HARVEY: So we have
this idea of fast autoscaling.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:29.700
And as we talked about,
that's elasticity.

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:30.920
So that's scaling up--

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:31.410
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:32.690
NATHEN HARVEY: --scaling down.

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:35.390
And the important thing there,
I think, in addition to fast

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:36.360
is autoscaling.

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:36.860
So--

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:37.430
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yes.

00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:39.680
NATHEN HARVEY: --it doesn't
require human intervention

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:40.670
or opening up a ticket.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.440
Hey, I need to scale
up because we're

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.280
about to have a bunch
of customers come in.

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:45.155
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:28:45.155 --> 00:28:46.130
Exactly.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:47.840
NATHEN HARVEY: Next, we
talked about cost visibility.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:48.830
NICOLE FORSGREN:
The cost visibility

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:49.405
is really interesting.

00:28:49.405 --> 00:28:49.680
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:51.380
NICOLE FORSGREN: So we do have
a section in the report showing

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:53.570
that because of the way
the cloud is architected,

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:56.210
and because of the information
it makes available,

00:28:56.210 --> 00:28:58.460
it brings that
visibility back to those

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:00.260
who are building systems.

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:02.790
Now, when we're on-prem, it's
just kind of a black box.

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:07.540
And so there is no incentive
to change the way that we're

00:29:07.540 --> 00:29:08.690
engineering our systems.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:13.210
If we want to aggressively make
our systems more efficient,

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:13.910
why?

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:14.950
And we can't.

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:18.550
The cloud enables us to do that,
in terms of compute resources

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:19.063
or cycles.

00:29:19.063 --> 00:29:20.230
NATHEN HARVEY: Right, right.

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:20.867
Interesting.

00:29:20.867 --> 00:29:23.200
And then you also mentioned
security here as a clear win

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:24.100
from cloud.

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:25.870
I know a lot of
people, as they're just

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:29.140
getting into the cloud, and
certainly in some industries,

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:31.900
there's some hesitance about
going to the cloud because

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:32.660
of security.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:34.910
So how is security a clear
win from the cloud, Nicole?

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.118
NICOLE FORSGREN: So there
are so many cloud providers

00:29:37.118 --> 00:29:39.850
that have already gone through
so many steps and so many hoops

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:43.480
to be a secure system that
meet so many compliance

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:46.870
regulations from
information security

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:48.700
to even physical security.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:51.010
But then you no
longer have to worry

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:53.260
about-- you can
allow your engineers

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:57.460
and your professionals to focus
on building code the right way,

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:59.720
and not have to worry
about securing your system

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:01.780
and securing your
buildings, even.

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:02.950
NATHEN HARVEY: Right, right.

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:04.250
Well, that's great.

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.610
And, of course,
Google Cloud has a ton

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:08.320
of different
services for you that

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:11.710
are available to allow
your engineers to focus

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:15.078
on building out the applications
that your customers care about.

00:30:15.078 --> 00:30:16.870
It turns out, your
customers don't actually

00:30:16.870 --> 00:30:19.690
care about the infrastructure
on which your applications run.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:22.170
What they care about is
that that infrastructure

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:22.930
is available.

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.120
Sure, they care about that.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:26.495
But they don't even care really
about the infrastructure.

00:30:26.495 --> 00:30:27.940
It's about the applications.

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:31.210
So Google Cloud offers a
number of different resources

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:35.020
that allow you to focus
exclusively on that application

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.720
and put some of those
infrastructure concerns

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:40.060
to the side, or
at least decrease

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:42.400
your investment in those
infrastructure concerns.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:45.040
So we have tools for
compute, for machine learning

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:49.330
and artificial intelligence,
messaging, monitoring,

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:52.240
learning-- all of these things
are built in to the Cloud

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:54.560
itself.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:58.930
And when we look
at the Cloud, we

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:00.930
see a number of
different capabilities

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:01.930
that are really awesome.

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:04.090
One of them that I'm
super excited about right

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:08.290
now is this function or
feature called Cloud Run.

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:12.220
Cloud Run is this idea where
we can take serverless agility

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:15.260
and use that with our
containerized applications.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:16.850
So what does that actually mean?

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:20.140
It means that our developers can
have a true serverless workflow

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:22.240
and serverless approach
to the applications

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:23.330
that they're building.

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:25.720
You take your application,
you containerize it,

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:27.190
and then you hand
it to Cloud Run.

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:27.940
That's it.

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:30.315
You don't have to worry about
your infrastructure at all.

00:31:30.315 --> 00:31:33.520
Cloud Run will scale up as
your users use your service

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:35.470
or as your demand requires.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:38.230
Cloud Run will also scale
all the way down to zero.

00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:41.050
And one of my favorite parts
about it, and my CFO's,

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:44.620
is you only pay for when
the requests are actually

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:45.580
being processed.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:46.070
NICOLE FORSGREN: Nice.

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:46.940
NATHEN HARVEY: Now,
that's really cool.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:49.870
It's also a great example of
something else that we've seen.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:53.410
We're seeing lots of
organizations, for example,

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:57.250
at the Broad Institute, they're
cutting the cost of research

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:00.070
by adopting more and more
of the Cloud features.

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:03.490
So maybe they started off
with just compute instances.

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:05.470
And, over time, as
they start really

00:32:05.470 --> 00:32:08.260
looking at the capabilities
that Google Cloud provides,

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:11.380
they're able to really
slash the cost of research.

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:14.050
So they go from maybe just
a compute instance down

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:17.277
to some task splitting, some
pre-emptable VMs, and so forth,

00:32:17.277 --> 00:32:19.360
all the way through to the
point where they're now

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:23.140
using algorithms to tune how
they're utilizing resources

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:24.265
on Google Cloud platform.

00:32:24.265 --> 00:32:26.890
NICOLE FORSGREN: So I love that,
because that's a great example

00:32:26.890 --> 00:32:29.650
of aligning incentives.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:33.950
Once you provide the
cloud to your developers,

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:37.550
they can really tease
apart only what they need.

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:41.110
They can build their
algorithms, their applications

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:43.505
to really be super efficient
and just use what they need.

00:32:43.505 --> 00:32:44.380
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:46.240
The other thing I
love about it is how

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:47.555
it took an iterative approach.

00:32:47.555 --> 00:32:48.430
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:32:48.430 --> 00:32:50.305
NATHEN HARVEY: Let's
get this into the cloud.

00:32:50.305 --> 00:32:53.320
And now that it's in cloud, we
can start thinking about, how

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:55.690
do we take more
advantage of the features

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:57.485
that the cloud really
has to offer for us?

00:32:57.485 --> 00:32:58.610
NICOLE FORSGREN: I love it.

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:00.527
And I love it because
it's the Broad Institute

00:33:00.527 --> 00:33:01.690
and they're doing research.

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:04.260
NATHEN HARVEY: Absolutely.

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:07.040
And the other thing that we talk
about a lot is shifting left.

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:09.160
So what does it mean
to shift left, Nicole?

00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:10.160
Can you talk about that?

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.077
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:33:11.077 --> 00:33:15.037
Shifting left is integrating
features and functions earlier

00:33:15.037 --> 00:33:15.870
into the tool chain.

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:17.078
And earlier into the process.

00:33:17.078 --> 00:33:19.090
Because if we find it late--

00:33:19.090 --> 00:33:20.690
late in the system,
late in the game--

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:21.180
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.430
NICOLE FORSGREN: --it's so
hard and it's so expensive.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.038
And by expensive,
I just mean effort

00:33:25.038 --> 00:33:26.330
to integrate all those changes.

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:29.187
The earlier we can find, the
earlier in the feedback cycle--

00:33:29.187 --> 00:33:30.020
NATHEN HARVEY: Right

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:31.050
NICOLE FORSGREN: --the
better we can deal with it.

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:32.270
And the higher the quality is.

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:32.510
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:33:32.510 --> 00:33:33.975
And so we've learned about that.

00:33:33.975 --> 00:33:35.600
When it comes to
building applications,

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:38.090
we all know that fixing
a bug in production

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:40.792
is much more expensive than
fixing a bug during design.

00:33:40.792 --> 00:33:41.750
NICOLE FORSGREN: Right.

00:33:41.750 --> 00:33:44.210
And in the 2018 report, we
found that elite performers

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:47.630
could find security features
in days and fix them in days.

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:49.880
Where the low performance,
it took them weeks to find.

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:51.770
And then weeks again
to remediate them.

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:52.070
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:53.630
So when security
is an afterthought,

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:55.130
when we think
about security when

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:57.870
we get into production, that
it's already too late, right?

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:58.070
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:33:58.070 --> 00:33:59.570
NATHEN HARVEY: So when we
talk about shifting left

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:01.160
on security, we're
really talking

00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:03.020
about taking those
security concerns

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:05.360
and moving them to the
left side of our workflow.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:10.190
We're back to the code, the
build portion of that workflow.

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:12.710
And, in fact, using
Google Cloud, when

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:15.900
you take your application and
build it into a container,

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:18.645
we have vulnerability scanning
that can look at that container

00:34:18.645 --> 00:34:20.020
and tell you
whether or not there

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:22.760
are security vulnerabilities,
known vulnerabilities,

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.730
before your container, before
your code gets anywhere

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:27.020
near production.

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:29.060
We can really improve
that feedback cycle

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:30.830
and get it earlier
in the process.

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:32.510
And so if there's a
vulnerability found,

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:33.929
we'll stop you there.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:36.889
And then the other thing that
we have is binary authorization.

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:39.542
So you've packaged up your
application into a container,

00:34:39.542 --> 00:34:41.000
you're ready to
take that container

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:45.080
and run it in your GKE cluster
or run it on Kubernetes.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.540
Well, how do you know that
that container that you're

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.389
about to run is actually the
container that you expect?

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:53.460
Is it an approved container
that you're going to run?

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:56.480
And so that's what binary
authorization can do for us.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:58.850
It will look at the
binaries and say, yes,

00:34:58.850 --> 00:35:00.650
this is an approved
binary or, no,

00:35:00.650 --> 00:35:02.820
this cannot go to production.

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:08.060
And we see this with tremendous
success over at Shopify.

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:12.170
So every day, 6,000
built container images

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:15.590
are deployed securely into
Shopify's environment.

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:18.320
6,000 container images.

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:22.820
And there are
actually over 3,300--

00:35:22.820 --> 00:35:27.948
sorry, 330,000 images stored in
Shopify's Container Registry.

00:35:27.948 --> 00:35:29.990
NICOLE FORSGREN: And I
love that because it shows

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:32.438
how security can be scalable.

00:35:32.438 --> 00:35:33.230
NATHEN HARVEY: Yes.

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:34.220
Absolutely.

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:35.802
And when we shift
it to the left,

00:35:35.802 --> 00:35:37.260
it makes it all
that more scalable.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:37.520
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:38.540
Exactly.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:41.030
Now-- OK, let's dig
into a few more.

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:42.530
The thing that's
important here-- so

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:45.330
think about a holistic
approach to CD.

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:46.580
Because it is technology.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:47.330
It is tooling.

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:47.660
NATHEN HARVEY: Yes.

00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:49.340
NICOLE FORSGREN:
It's also process.

00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:51.200
So when we take a
look here, we can

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:53.810
see how all of these things,
clear change process,

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:56.060
disaster recovery testing,
continuous delivering

00:35:56.060 --> 00:35:59.510
in this automation, really
drives our speed, stability,

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:02.130
and availability.

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:03.990
One thing that's
important here is,

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:08.460
we think about useful,
integrated, easy-to-use tools

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.723
it helps makes CI/CD accessible.

00:36:10.723 --> 00:36:13.140
Now, on a personal note, I
love this, because some of this

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:16.320
reaches all the way back to my
dissertation and my postdoc.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:18.000
NATHEN HARVEY: Nice.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:20.670
Well, when you think about the
integrated experience-- look,

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:22.150
I'm a developer.

00:36:22.150 --> 00:36:25.730
I want to stay in the same
context throughout my day.

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:28.230
And it doesn't matter if I'm
writing code for the first time

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:30.900
or getting that code
pushed off to production.

00:36:30.900 --> 00:36:33.000
And so with things
like Cloud Build, which

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:36.750
is integrated with GitHub, you
can actually get those signals

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:39.870
all without leaving the
UI and the experience

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:41.050
that you're used to.

00:36:41.050 --> 00:36:44.022
So within GitHub itself,
when I submit a change,

00:36:44.022 --> 00:36:45.480
Cloud Build can
pick up that change

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:46.605
and automatically build it.

00:36:46.605 --> 00:36:48.600
I don't have to switch
contexts and think,

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.210
OK, I've submitted a change, now
I need to go build the thing.

00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:53.280
The thing gets built.
And I get feedback

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:55.860
directly in that same
interface that tells me,

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:56.940
how did that build go?

00:36:56.940 --> 00:36:58.740
Were there vulnerabilities
in that build?

00:36:58.740 --> 00:36:59.752
And so forth.

00:36:59.752 --> 00:37:01.080
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:04.580
Now, what we found is only
40% of our respondents

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:08.100
are doing disaster
recovery testing annually.

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:09.587
NATHEN HARVEY: Annually?

00:37:09.587 --> 00:37:10.670
NICOLE FORSGREN: Annually.

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:12.110
NATHEN HARVEY: Annually.

00:37:12.110 --> 00:37:13.340
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Disaster recovery testing

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:14.840
is pretty important
because it helps

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:17.720
us understand the state
our production systems

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:20.280
and what's necessary,
important to keep them running.

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:23.870
So I love the idea of these
DiRT events-- disaster recovery

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:24.720
testing.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:26.407
And before I ever
came to Google,

00:37:26.407 --> 00:37:27.740
I kept hearing stories about it.

00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:31.580
So much so that we included
this quote from Kripa Krishnan

00:37:31.580 --> 00:37:32.340
in the book.

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:34.007
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah,
it's a great quote.

00:37:34.007 --> 00:37:35.950
For DiRT-style events
to be successful,

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:39.320
an organization first needs
to accept system and process

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:42.170
failures as a means of learning.

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:44.240
Things will go wrong.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:47.330
When they do, the focus needs
to be on fixing the error

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:49.610
instead of reprimanding
an individual

00:37:49.610 --> 00:37:53.580
or team for a failure
of complex systems.

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:54.600
I love that.

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:57.390
The whole focus on
learning is something that

00:37:57.390 --> 00:37:58.640
really brings joy to my heart.

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:00.140
NICOLE FORSGREN: I
know, it's great.

00:38:00.140 --> 00:38:01.740
And the thing that
I love about this

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:03.660
is that focus is not
just on improving

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:07.025
the technology, but also
the team surrounding it

00:38:07.025 --> 00:38:08.400
and the processes
surrounding it.

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:09.858
Because there's so
much opportunity

00:38:09.858 --> 00:38:11.130
to make things better here.

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:12.560
NATHEN HARVEY: Yes.

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:14.540
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now,
I'm excited about this.

00:38:14.540 --> 00:38:17.900
Because in the past,
Google's Project Aristotle

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:20.580
has studied this culture
of psychological safety

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:23.030
and how it's so important for
teams to be able to do this.

00:38:23.030 --> 00:38:23.550
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.175
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now, there was
a little bit of side commentary

00:38:26.175 --> 00:38:30.530
out in the industry saying, of
course, Google would find this.

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:34.670
Because Google is
this environment where

00:38:34.670 --> 00:38:36.950
you have this amazing
culture, and you

00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:38.630
don't have to
worry about things,

00:38:38.630 --> 00:38:41.390
and they give you
lunch, and everyone

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:45.163
passes this amazingly
difficult bar for interview.

00:38:45.163 --> 00:38:46.580
And there's no way
that this could

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:49.290
hold in the rest of the world.

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:52.550
And so many thanks to the
Project Aristotle team.

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:54.860
They shared their
measurement instrument

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:58.200
with the DORA research
team this year.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.970
And so we studied
this externally.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:01.410
And this is what we found.

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:07.940
We found that this same culture
of openness and shared purpose

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:11.108
also contributes to software
development, delivery.

00:39:11.108 --> 00:39:11.900
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:12.560
NICOLE FORSGREN: So
it's a slightly--

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:14.505
it's a different
outcome, but similar.

00:39:14.505 --> 00:39:15.380
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:39:15.380 --> 00:39:17.340
NICOLE FORSGREN: In
the rest of the world,

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:19.515
these same findings hold true.

00:39:19.515 --> 00:39:20.390
NATHEN HARVEY: I see.

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:22.553
And culture is more
than just free lunch.

00:39:22.553 --> 00:39:23.720
NICOLE FORSGREN: Absolutely.

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:25.130
NATHEN HARVEY: So let's talk
about some of the culture

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.710
that we do have a Google
that I think can apply

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:29.580
in your organization as well.

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:31.560
So the first is that
we let data guide us.

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:33.680
We use data to make decisions.

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:35.060
That's super important.

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:37.682
Next, we have collaboration
and a shared fate.

00:39:37.682 --> 00:39:39.140
So when you think
about DevOps, how

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:41.330
do we align development
and operations?

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:42.720
We're collaborating together.

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:46.325
We're sharing a fate or we're
sharing responsibilities.

00:39:46.325 --> 00:39:47.450
Next, there's transparency.

00:39:47.450 --> 00:39:49.825
So we talked about this a lot
when we talked about Cloud.

00:39:49.825 --> 00:39:53.210
Can you get transparency
into what is the resource?

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:54.890
What are the
resources costing you?

00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:56.430
How are those
resources performing?

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:57.110
And so forth.

00:39:57.110 --> 00:40:01.400
That transparency is
important within our systems.

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.440
But when I say systems,
I mean our systems.

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:06.020
Not just our systems,
but our systems, right?

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:08.070
So think about CPU, of course.

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:10.365
But finances and
customer satisfaction.

00:40:10.365 --> 00:40:11.240
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:40:11.240 --> 00:40:13.800
NATHEN HARVEY: Transparency is
important across all of that.

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:15.920
And then, lastly, and
every organization

00:40:15.920 --> 00:40:18.230
can take on this as
part of their culture,

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:20.210
a blameless post-mortem culture.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.660
Where when incidents happen,
and they will happen, what we do

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:26.720
is we take those incidents and
those failures as opportunities

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:27.500
to learn.

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:31.107
And we do that through a good
blameless post-mortem culture.

00:40:31.107 --> 00:40:31.940
NICOLE FORSGREN: OK.

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:35.090
Now, let's dig into the
second research model.

00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:36.530
As I said before,
any time there's

00:40:36.530 --> 00:40:38.060
a box, that's a construct.

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:38.450
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:40:38.450 --> 00:40:39.260
NICOLE FORSGREN: Any
time there's an arrow,

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:40.790
you can read it as a prediction.

00:40:40.790 --> 00:40:43.310
If that error has a minus,
it means it reduces.

00:40:43.310 --> 00:40:43.550
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:45.650
NICOLE FORSGREN: So this year,
we took a look at productivity.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:47.780
Now, this is a special
kind of productivity.

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:49.890
It's not writing lines of code.

00:40:49.890 --> 00:40:52.220
It's not the number
of bugs that you kill.

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:54.860
It's when you hit that
special flow, when

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:57.140
you feel like you've done
a lot of good, complex work

00:40:57.140 --> 00:40:58.130
for the day.

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:00.478
Now, productivity is
important because it

00:41:00.478 --> 00:41:02.270
helps us feel like
we've accomplished work.

00:41:02.270 --> 00:41:05.270
It helps us do a lot of
good work for the day.

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:08.790
Now, what types of things do
we feel I contribute to this?

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:11.420
We just talked about
useful easy-to-use tools.

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:14.240
This is great because it's
highly correlated to the CI/CD

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:16.070
work that we just talked about.

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:17.420
Also search.

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:20.015
Also things like
reducing technical debt.

00:41:20.015 --> 00:41:20.890
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:22.760
NICOLE FORSGREN: We're going
to dig into a couple of these.

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:22.954
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:41:22.954 --> 00:41:23.150
So it's--

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:24.410
NICOLE FORSGREN: I just
talked about search.

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:24.620
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:26.040
Let's start with search.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:30.200
So I see here that
we have a 1.73 times

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:33.595
productivity improvement when
there's good internal search.

00:41:33.595 --> 00:41:34.970
What you mean by
internal search?

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:36.620
NICOLE FORSGREN: So internal
search encompasses a lot

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:37.120
of things.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:39.290
It's like, can I search
my internal code base?

00:41:39.290 --> 00:41:42.650
Do I have a good internal
set of documentation?

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:44.330
Do I have knowledge bases?

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:45.170
Do I have wikis?

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:47.440
Can I-- do I feel like
I can ask my colleagues?

00:41:47.440 --> 00:41:48.190
NATHEN HARVEY: Hm.

00:41:48.190 --> 00:41:48.410
OK.

00:41:48.410 --> 00:41:49.690
NICOLE FORSGREN: It
encompasses a few things.

00:41:49.690 --> 00:41:50.000
NATHEN HARVEY: Got you.

00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:52.610
And then external search might
be things like Stack Overflow

00:41:52.610 --> 00:41:54.620
or YouTube videos,
for that matter,

00:41:54.620 --> 00:41:57.770
places where developers and
operators go to learn more

00:41:57.770 --> 00:41:59.692
about the technology
that they're using,

00:41:59.692 --> 00:42:02.150
or the tools that they're using,
maybe the bug that they've

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:02.860
encountered--

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:03.110
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:04.040
NATHEN HARVEY: --they use
external search for that.

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:05.690
NICOLE FORSGREN: One note
that's important to point out

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:07.310
here is that external
search is going

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:09.620
to be better if you're
using open source tools,

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:11.870
because there will be a broad
community from which you

00:42:11.870 --> 00:42:12.530
can pull.

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:12.920
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:13.580
For sure.

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:17.135
And who isn't using open source?

00:42:17.135 --> 00:42:19.760
NICOLE FORSGREN: I will say that
low performers are the highest

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:21.650
users of a fully
proprietary toolset.

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:22.400
NATHEN HARVEY: Oh.

00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:23.270
NICOLE FORSGREN: But
read into it more.

00:42:23.270 --> 00:42:24.500
We don't have time
to get into it now.

00:42:24.500 --> 00:42:24.860
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah, definitely.

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:25.990
NICOLE FORSGREN: Teaser.

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:26.650
OK.

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:31.260
Also technical debt drives
reductions in productivity.

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:32.810
So how can we reduce
technical debt?

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:34.040
This is super important.

00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:36.350
And we see the shops
so many places.

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:38.360
One thing that's interesting--

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:40.160
because we all know
that refactoring

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:41.330
helps with technical debt.

00:42:41.330 --> 00:42:43.200
Martin Fowler wrote
a great book on it.

00:42:43.200 --> 00:42:46.100
But what else can we do in
terms of smart investments

00:42:46.100 --> 00:42:47.480
in tooling?

00:42:47.480 --> 00:42:49.220
Code maintainability?

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:50.840
How can we maintain our code?

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.640
Help our code
bases be searchable

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:57.050
so that we don't contribute
to tough dependencies?

00:42:57.050 --> 00:42:59.720
Loosely coupled architecture
helps with this, as well as

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:01.310
monitoring.

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:03.710
One thing that's interesting,
you'll probably note,

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:08.000
these things also show up
in our other research model.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:13.440
Smart investments in flexible,
extensible viewable systems

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:14.565
help reduce technical debt.

00:43:14.565 --> 00:43:16.565
NATHEN HARVEY: So this
can help with performance

00:43:16.565 --> 00:43:17.310
and productivity.

00:43:17.310 --> 00:43:18.420
NICOLE FORSGREN: Absolutely.

00:43:18.420 --> 00:43:18.930
NATHEN HARVEY: Great.

00:43:18.930 --> 00:43:20.263
NICOLE FORSGREN: Now, what else?

00:43:20.263 --> 00:43:21.090
Why do we care?

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:22.990
I mean, I want to be productive.

00:43:22.990 --> 00:43:24.630
The other reason
this is important

00:43:24.630 --> 00:43:27.450
is that, when I have
a good day at work,

00:43:27.450 --> 00:43:29.550
when I feel like I hit
that great information,

00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:30.857
that great flow--

00:43:30.857 --> 00:43:31.690
NATHEN HARVEY: Yeah.

00:43:31.690 --> 00:43:33.523
NICOLE FORSGREN: --and
I can get a lot done,

00:43:33.523 --> 00:43:34.980
I can solve complex
problems, that

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:37.310
lets me leave work at work.

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:38.910
I can shut my head off.

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:40.710
I can shut my brain off.

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:43.997
I can go home and it
helps reduce burnout.

00:43:43.997 --> 00:43:45.330
NATHEN HARVEY: Oh, that's great.

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:47.690
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yeah.

00:43:47.690 --> 00:43:52.895
So next up, quickly, what
works and what doesn't.

00:43:52.895 --> 00:43:54.270
So many people
ask me, how can we

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:56.010
scale my DevOps transformation?

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:57.010
NATHEN HARVEY: For sure.

00:43:57.010 --> 00:43:59.400
I mean, we've been talking about
the word DevOps for a decade

00:43:59.400 --> 00:43:59.790
now.

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:00.150
NICOLE FORSGREN: I know.

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:02.310
NATHEN HARVEY: And
I've seen organizations

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:04.140
where a small pocket
of the organization

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.670
will adopt some of these
practices and ideas,

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:09.480
and they do so to great success.

00:44:09.480 --> 00:44:12.675
But how do they get that to go
across the entire organization?

00:44:12.675 --> 00:44:14.050
How do you actually
scale it out?

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:14.530
NICOLE FORSGREN: Right.

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:15.960
So we asked all
of our respondents

00:44:15.960 --> 00:44:17.710
what they're doing,
what they were seeing.

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:20.130
And then we did an additional
analysis, another cluster

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:23.530
analysis, particularly among
our high and elite performers,

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:25.840
which was over 40%
of our population.

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:28.810
So you can choose what
works best for you.

00:44:28.810 --> 00:44:29.670
Here's what we see.

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:34.050
Elite teams favor structural
solutions that build community.

00:44:34.050 --> 00:44:38.560
Things like communities of
practice, grassroots efforts,

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:40.260
and a couple proof of concepts.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:43.530
The reasons these are good is
because they build communities

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:47.820
so they will be robust to things
like reorgs or product changes.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:48.570
NATHEN HARVEY: OK.

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:50.490
So if these are the
things that are good,

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.410
what are the things
that we should avoid?

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.720
NICOLE FORSGREN: These
things are not great--

00:44:54.720 --> 00:44:58.050
training centers,
centers of excellence,

00:44:58.050 --> 00:44:59.680
or a big bang approach.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:02.670
Now, there were a
couple notable training

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:05.448
centers that have gotten some
press that are pretty good.

00:45:05.448 --> 00:45:06.240
NATHEN HARVEY: Mhm.

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:08.120
NICOLE FORSGREN: The
challenge of some of these,

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:09.810
and centers of
excellence, are that it

00:45:09.810 --> 00:45:12.210
centers expertise in one place.

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.970
It essentially
creates another silo.

00:45:14.970 --> 00:45:18.370
They also take
extensive resources.

00:45:18.370 --> 00:45:20.190
It's really, really
tough to figure out

00:45:20.190 --> 00:45:22.380
how to get that to spread
throughout an organization

00:45:22.380 --> 00:45:23.485
and keep it sustainable.

00:45:23.485 --> 00:45:24.360
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:26.310
NICOLE FORSGREN: It can work.

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:28.060
But you've got to know
what you're in for.

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:31.140
NATHEN HARVEY: Well, and I would
suspect that a training center

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:33.780
works best when the learners
that come into that training

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:36.285
center come in with a problem
of their own to solve--

00:45:36.285 --> 00:45:36.570
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:38.153
NATHEN HARVEY: --as
opposed to come in

00:45:38.153 --> 00:45:40.345
to consume some
predefined curriculum.

00:45:40.345 --> 00:45:41.220
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:43.830
Now, we do see it show up in a
handful of our high performers.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:45.030
But only about 9%.

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:45.780
NATHEN HARVEY: Mm.

00:45:45.780 --> 00:45:47.280
NICOLE FORSGREN:
Now, I will mention

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:49.440
big bang approach doesn't
show up among our highest

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:51.880
performers really at all.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:54.100
We do see it among
our low performers,

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:55.050
which does make sense.

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:57.690
If you need a hard
reset, you can do it.

00:45:57.690 --> 00:45:59.190
But you need to
know that this needs

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:01.890
to be a multi-year approach
and a multi-year model.

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:03.897
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:46:03.897 --> 00:46:04.730
NICOLE FORSGREN: OK.

00:46:04.730 --> 00:46:06.620
Some of our key takeaways.

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:09.290
The industry continues
to approve, particularly

00:46:09.290 --> 00:46:11.098
among our highest performers.

00:46:11.098 --> 00:46:12.140
NATHEN HARVEY: All right.

00:46:12.140 --> 00:46:12.932
Well, that's great.

00:46:12.932 --> 00:46:14.775
So if the industry
continues to improve--

00:46:14.775 --> 00:46:17.182
and we talked earlier about
DevOps cross the chasm.

00:46:17.182 --> 00:46:18.765
Can we just stop
talking about DevOps?

00:46:18.765 --> 00:46:19.150
NICOLE FORSGREN: [LAUGH]

00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:20.932
NATHEN HARVEY: Is
it now just the way?

00:46:20.932 --> 00:46:22.140
NICOLE FORSGREN: It could be.

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:26.060
And honestly, so many companies
I work with ask me that.

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:28.520
I would say, choose
the word that resonates

00:46:28.520 --> 00:46:29.840
for you and your organization.

00:46:29.840 --> 00:46:33.260
If it's DevOps, if it's agile,
if it's digital transformation,

00:46:33.260 --> 00:46:35.420
if it's just
transformation, use whatever

00:46:35.420 --> 00:46:36.780
is going to work for you.

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:37.520
NATHEN HARVEY: And
that's going to help you

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:39.853
with both your commercial and
your non-commercial goals.

00:46:39.853 --> 00:46:40.728
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:46:40.728 --> 00:46:41.600
Absolutely.

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:43.250
Cloud continues to
be a differentiator

00:46:43.250 --> 00:46:44.810
for the elite performers.

00:46:44.810 --> 00:46:46.490
But, remember, you
have to do it right.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:46.740
NATHEN HARVEY: Right.

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:49.550
So make those investments
in those five key areas that

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:52.300
define, what does it mean to
actually be using the cloud?

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:53.850
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:55.850
Productivity can drive
performance and work/life

00:46:55.850 --> 00:46:58.290
balance and reduce burnout.

00:46:58.290 --> 00:47:00.530
NATHEN HARVEY: Which
is super important.

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:01.880
NICOLE FORSGREN: For sure.

00:47:01.880 --> 00:47:04.100
NATHEN HARVEY: And also the
bottom-up or grassroots,

00:47:04.100 --> 00:47:05.990
along with communities
of practice,

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:09.010
work well for scaling out
your DevOps transformation.

00:47:09.010 --> 00:47:10.580
NICOLE FORSGREN: Yep.

00:47:10.580 --> 00:47:12.530
Now, remember,
download the report

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:14.630
for so many more
findings and details.

00:47:14.630 --> 00:47:17.972
You can find that at
cloud.google.com/devops.

00:47:17.972 --> 00:47:19.680
NATHEN HARVEY: That's
the 2019 Accelerate

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:21.510
State of DevOps Report.

00:47:21.510 --> 00:47:22.010
All right.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:24.410
Well, thank you so much
for tuning in today.

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:27.860
Please visit "Cloud OnAir" for
more content from Google Cloud

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:29.280
experts.

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:29.830
Bye-bye.

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:31.030
NICOLE FORSGREN: Thanks.

00:47:31.030 --> 00:47:34.380
